  "_error_map_note": "Reference: monday.com 'Error codes' (developer.monday.com/api-reference/docs/errors), the published table of error code + HTTP status. PAYLOAD SHAPE: a handled monday failure answers {\"error_code\": \"<code>\", \"status_code\": <int>, \"error_message\": \"...\", \"error_data\": {...}} -- a top-level 'error_code', which core.pull_errors._extract_provider_codes already reads first among top-level keys, so NO normalisation is needed here and the keys are the documented codes verbatim. ONLY the codes carried on a NON-2xx status are keyed: monday's GraphQL-layer codes (COMPLEXITY_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED, UNAUTHENTICATED, ...) ride on HTTP 200 and are handled in connector.graphql_request + connector._ERROR_CODES, where they belong -- a 200 never reaches classify_http_error, so keying them here would be a dead key (the AI-114 defect). WHAT THE KEYS BUY: '401:Unauthorized' becomes auth_revoked rather than auth_expired -- a monday OAuth access token has no expiry and there is no refresh token, so a rejected token is one the user or the admin revoked and the repair is to reconnect, not to wait for a refresh; '401:Your ip is restricted' becomes permission_denied, because reconnecting cannot lift an account IP allowlist and telling the operator to reconnect would send them round a loop; '404:ResourceNotFoundException' becomes permission_denied because the published table reads 'invalid user ID OR insufficient access' and core would otherwise leave it 'unclassified', i.e. retried forever. THE 401/403 BRANCHES NOW ROUTE THROUGH classify_http_error: they used to raise MondayGraphQLError('auth_expired'/'permission_denied') before core saw them, so no key on those statuses could ever have fired. 429 and the rate-limit codes keep the core.quota.RateLimitError breaker path and are never keyed here. Remaining and NOT smuggled in here: MondayGraphQLError still carries non-canonical class strings (rate_limited, resource_not_found, provider_error, invalid_response) on the HTTP 200 path. ORIGINAL AI-114 NOTE (2026-08-01, kept for the record): monday is the case where the contract itself cannot express the vocabulary -- not a case where nobody looked. monday is a GraphQL API: it answers HTTP 200 and puts the failure in errors[].extensions.code (COMPLEXITY_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED, DAILY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED, MINUTE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED, CONCURRENCY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED, IP_RATE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED, UNAUTHENTICATED, AUTHENTICATION_ERROR, FORBIDDEN, PERMISSION_DENIED, NOT_FOUND, RESOURCE_PROTECTION). error_map's grammar is '<status>:<provider_code>' and its reader is core.pull_errors.classify_http_error, which is only ever called on a NON-2xx response -- so there is no HTTP status to key these codes on, and the eleven bare keys removed here could never be read by anything. They were also a verbatim duplicate of connector._ERROR_CODES, which is the live mapping and the one to edit; and two of their values (rate_limited, resource_not_found) are not in core.pull_errors.ERROR_CLASSES at all, so classify_http_error would have dropped them in silence even with a readable key. WHAT IS STILL WRONG, AND IT IS NOT THIS FILE: connector.MondayGraphQLError carries its own class strings (rate_limited, resource_not_found, provider_error, invalid_response) instead of the canonical taxonomy, so a monday failure reaches the worker without a canonical error_class and the per-class retry policy cannot apply. Rewiring it is a story of its own (the rate-limit path feeds the quota breaker and is pinned by tests); it must not be smuggled in as a manifest edit.",
